MITRE ATT&CK Journey

Tactic #6: Privilege Escalation

🎯 Goal
The attacker's goal is to gain higher-level permissions on a compromised system.

One-line definition:
Privilege Escalation is the process of obtaining higher privileges (such as Administrator or SYSTEM) after gaining initial access to a system.


🏠 Real-life Example

Imagine a visitor enters an office building with a guest pass.

They can enter the lobby.
They cannot enter the server room.

If they steal the manager's access card, they can now enter every restricted area.
➡️ That is Privilege Escalation.


💻 Cybersecurity Example

An attacker successfully compromises a normal user account.
Instead of staying as a normal user, they attempt to become:

Administrator
Root (Linux)
SYSTEM (Windows)

Once successful, they can:

Disable antivirus
Create new admin users
Dump passwords
Install malware
Control the entire machine


🔴 Common MITRE Techniques

Here are some common techniques used for Privilege Escalation:

1. Exploiting Vulnerabilities
Attackers exploit an operating system or software vulnerability to become Administrator.

Example:

Windows Local Privilege Escalation vulnerability
Linux kernel vulnerability


2. Credential Dumping

Stealing credentials from memory.
Popular tool:
Mimikatz
Attackers dump passwords and hashes from memory.


3. Abuse of Misconfigured Permissions

Examples:

Weak folder permissions
Writable service files
Unquoted service paths
Weak scheduled task permissions

4. Token Impersonation

Windows access tokens are stolen and reused to impersonate another user.


5. Bypassing UAC (User Account Control)

The attacker tricks Windows into granting Administrator privileges without a normal prompt.


🚩 SOC Analyst Red Flags

If you're monitoring logs, investigate when you see:

🚩 A normal user suddenly becomes Administrator.

🚩 Unexpected use of:

runas
psexec
wmic
powershell

🚩 Creation of new administrator accounts.

🚩 Suspicious service modifications.

🚩 Scheduled tasks created by unusual users.

🚩 Multiple privilege assignment events.


📄 Windows Event IDs to Monitor
Some important Windows Security Event IDs include:
| Event ID | Meaning                                    |
| -------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| 4672     | Special privileges assigned to a new logon |
| 4688     | New process created                        |
| 4720     | User account created                       |
| 4728     | User added to a privileged group           |
| 4732     | Member added to a local security group     |
| 7045     | New service installed (System log)         |


🔍 SOC Investigation Questions
When investigating Privilege Escalation, ask yourself:

Why was Administrator privilege requested?
Which user executed the command?
Which process launched it?
Was PowerShell involved?
Was cmd.exe started by an Office application?
Did the user recently log in from a new location?
Were new administrator accounts created?
Was a service installed unexpectedly?


🧠 Remember:
Privilege Escalation is about getting more permissions, not about keeping access.

Privilege Escalation = Gain higher privileges.
Persistence = Maintain access over time.



